Build Specification — App 33: The Learning Provenance System
Completeness status: COMPLETE. All sections delivered in one pass. No truncation. Formatted for copy-paste. Long lists are broken into short lines.

1. What It Is
A structured record of what a student has learned, how they learned it, and how it connects to what they learned before. Each entry states the concept, the evidence of understanding, the prior concepts it builds on, and the related concepts. The Learning Provenance System is a provenance-aware graph of a student's knowledge, where each node is a concept and each edge is a relation: prerequisite, builds-on, related, or applied-in. It records not just what a student knows, but how each piece of knowledge connects to the others and what evidence supports it.

It reuses:

the STAG Annotation Editor (App 1)

the STAG Schema Library (App 17)

the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20)

the Revision History Viewer (App 7)

the Method Tracker (App 32)

the STAG Graph Viewer (App 5)

the Autofill Library Service (App 49)

It is for students, teachers, tutors, learning scientists, and anyone who wants to track understanding rather than grades.

2. Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept deposits of learning records from users.

Store each learning record with the concept, the evidence of understanding, and the provenance.

Record the evidence of understanding: the assessment, the demonstration, the application, the explanation.

Record the prior concepts: the concepts that must be learned before this one.

Record the related concepts: the concepts that are connected but not prerequisites.

Record the applied-in contexts: where the concept has been used.

Record the date learned and the date last reviewed.

Record the proficiency level for each concept.

Record the source of learning: the course, the book, the lecture, the video, the project, the experiment.

Record the relation between concepts: which ones are prerequisites, which build on others, which are alternatives, which are special cases.

Enforce provenance completeness.

Enforce versioning.

Enforce supersession acyclicity.

Support querying by learning ID, concept, subject, proficiency level, student, date learned, date last reviewed, and keywords.

Support full-text search of the concept description, the evidence, and the notes.

Support aggregation queries: count by subject, by proficiency level, by student, by year, by source of learning.

Support exporting a learning record as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle.

Support importing a learning record from JSON.

Support bulk import and bulk export.

Support a REST API for CRUD operations.

Support a GraphQL API for flexible queries.

Support a command-line interface for administration.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Support a web interface.

Support moderation.

Support linking a learning record to a Method Tracker record if the concept is a method.

Support linking a learning record to a STAG annotation if the concept involves a scale-transition claim.

Support linking a learning record to a Physics Knowledge Library work if the concept is described in a deposited work.

Support the learning graph: a visualisation of the student's concepts and their relations.

Support the learning timeline: a visualisation of when each concept was learned and reviewed.

Support the knowledge gap view: a visualisation of the concepts that are missing or weak.

Support the learning path view: a suggested sequence of concepts to learn next.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic.

Fast: query a typical learning record in under 100 milliseconds.

Scalable: support up to 10^6 learning records.

Versioned: the system records the STAG schema version, the learning schema version, and the system version.

Reproducible.

Portable.

Well-documented.

